### The Lighthouse Keeper’s Burden  

The lighthouse stood at the edge of the stormy sea, its weathered stone walls and towering spire a testament to the passage of time. For over a century, it had served as a beacon of hope for sailors lost at sea, guiding them toward safety with its steady glow. But the lighthouse keeper, an aging man named Elias, had spent his life in this remote outpost, tending to the light and the people who relied on it. The sea was cruel, and the nights were long, but Elias had always found solace in the rhythm of the tides and the soft hum of the lantern’s flame.  

Elias was not a man of many words, but his presence was felt. He moved with quiet purpose, his hands calloused from years of cleaning the lenses, repairing the wiring, and maintaining the intricate mechanisms that kept the light burning. He had no family, no friends, and no future beyond the lighthouse. The world beyond the cliffs was a place of uncertainty, and the lighthouse was his only home. Yet, despite the solitude, he found strength in the responsibility he carried.  

The lighthouse was not just a place of light—it was a symbol of hope. And Elias, though old, had never lost that sense of purpose. He had seen the world change, the ships that once passed with ease now distant memories, and the people who had once depended on him now scattered across the world. But he had never let the weight of time slow him down. He had learned to trust the light, to let it guide him, and to find peace in the quiet certainty of its glow.

